ttee have  made their pledge the grove in norcross  I'm not quite sure what kind of progress  you're going to have senator let me ask  you about the the Democratic side though  of the six Democrats that have been  named  which ones which one might give you  pause that they could deal with the  other side and who do you have  confidence in well I certainly know all  the you know the three folks in the  Senate and they're all smart and strong  people like no they house folks as well  I would simply hope that the Democrats  hang together and that they demand  fairness and they demand policies that  help us create the kinds of jobs that we  desperately need in this economy but we  cannot continue to surrender it to the  Republicans who say yeah we have a  deficit problem but we don't want the  wealthiest people in this country  millionaires and billionaires to  contribute one penny more nor do we want  large profitable corporations that  contribute it has to come completely out  of the backs of working families the  elderly the sick and the children the  Democrats have got to say no that cannot  be the case that's not what the American  people want so I would hope the  Democrats hold firm and maybe for a  change the Republicans do some yelling  so if the Republicans were willing to  give up taxes and we're willing to to do  some of the things that you just talked  about would you be willing to see  reductions in Social Security Medicare  Medicaid as a trader always I think  there are all ways that you can move  toward deficit reduction but in fact I  don't think you have to hit those three  programs i think there are other  programs though i would prefer not to  see cut but i think may have to be cut  uh throughout across the board i will  tell you this that we have tripled  funding for defense since 1997 we're now  spending about a hundred and sixty  billion dollars a year still fighting  the wars in Iraq and Afghanistan we have  many weapons systems out there that were  designed to fight the old Cold War and  not international terrorism so I happen  to believe that we can make very  significant cuts in military spending as  part of deficit reduction while still  retaining our military strong military  presence there are other programs in  agriculture and across the board I think  you can cut but I don't know how you cut  Medicaid when you got 50 million people  today without  health insurance I don't know what you  do about Medicare Medicare you can make  some changes without reducing benefits  that I would be open to that for example  right now we don't negotiate drug prices  I think there's a lot of savings to be  made in Medicare if we did what we do  with the VA in negotiations with the  drug companies there are improvements  that can be made but the idea for  example that some have floated about  raising the eligibility level 267 is  basically insane what do you do if  you're 66 and your low income and you're  sick with cancer what does that mean  it's a death sentence and if I could not  support that senators and let me bring  you back to taxes as much as you say  that the Republicans are entrenched in  there no new taxes position colleague of  mine at Reuters spoke with Dave camp  yesterday the chairman of the House Ways  and Means Committee he said everything's  on the table as far as he's concerned  including tax policy Pat Toomey who is a  tea party favorite said yesterday that  there are quote indefensible tax breaks  that need to be looked at is there room  for some sort of maybe even symbolic tax  break closures here in this negotiation  well I would certainly hope so I'm glad  to hear some of these guys saying that  but I feared that at the end of the day  I may be wrong and I and I hope that  they are sincere about that but as you  well know the devil is in the details  what many of these guys believe in which  I don't is trickle-down economics and  that is a few lower tax rates for large  corporations which is wh