u for being our  newsmakers appreciate it thank you very  much let me turn to our reporters here  and in just Kevin if I can begin with  you what you hear from the senator and  the prospects for the super committee  that the all the members have been named  and and they're going to start with  their work well he's clearly frustrated  with the way the White House has been  handling this deficit confrontation over  the last year or so the line that we're  getting from Democrats in from Bernie  Sanders an independent seems to be to  hold the line and to try and protect  Medicare Medicaid Social Security these  giant social programs that are so  important to progressives and Democrats  it's he did seem to be open to the idea  that there might be some promise for  maybe chiefly symbolic tax reform to  come out of this process but I don't  know that there's a pessimistic outlook  on that yeah yeah Laura go ahead  what I took from this is that most of  what the debate around this whole  deficit-reduction question has been has  been that the assumption that Democrats  are willing to compromise and  Republicans are not Democrats led by  President Obama have said no we're  putting entitlements on the table you  somewhat reluctantly leader Pelosi and  Majority Leader Reid have gone along  with that and of course republicans have  not what he's basically saying is no no  no that's that's not how it's going to  be you know I'm speaking up for for the  left wing of the Democratic Party and  I'm not willing to make those  compromises so whether his view will  prevail at the end of the day you know  I'm certainly not predicting that I  think that chances are if this committee  comes up with a what Obama would call a  balanced deal with taxes and  entitlements that that probably will go  through but I think what he's trying to  do is and this is I think the role that  he typically does play in the Congress  is to provide some countervailing  pressure from the left to say that you  k