this week on newsmakers senator Bernie  Sanders independent of Vermont joining  us from that state but member of the  Budget Committee as well and to  reporters to help us with questions  Kevin Drawbaugh Reuters and Laura  Meckler of the Wall Street Journal Kevin  let me begin with you first question  senator Sanders before the all the names  for the super committee came out it felt  like there was a moment here in  Washington where people felt like this  committee might be able to achieve some  sort of grand bargain meaning you know  bipartisan agreement on taxes and  spending cuts but since the names have  come out mostly hardliners from the  Republicans and sort of policy  lieutenants from the Democrats the mood  is shifted back to you know maybe this  is just another round of deadlock what  do you think is this a breakthrough  moment or more the same well I look at  it a little bit differently the way I  see it is in the last three negotiations  that we had going back to December and  in terms of tax extensions in terms of  when the Republicans in April threatened  to shut down the government and then the  recent debt ceiling issue I think the  Republicans kind of won all three of  those negotiations hands down I think  most people consider that that to be the  case my view is that right now what the  Democrats have got to do is remain  strong I protect Social Security  Medicare and Medicaid and the  Republicans I believe I've got to do  with the overwhelming majority the  American people wanting in every poll  that we have seen and that is you have  to have shared sacrifice you have to  have the wealthiest people in this  country who are doing phenomenally well  and their effective tax rate is the  lowest in decades large corporations who  many cases are making billions in  profits sometimes pay nothing in taxes  they have got to contribute to deficit  reduction and if they choose not to do  that and I think you know almost all of  the Republicans on this committee have  made their pledge the grove in norcross  I'm not quite sure what kind of progress  you're going to have senator let me ask  you about the the Democratic side though  of the six Democrats that have been  named  which ones which one might give you  pause that they could deal with the  other side and who do you have  confidence in well I certainly know all  the you know the three folks in the  Senate and they're all smart and strong  people like no they house folks as well  I would simply hope that the Democrats  hang together and that they demand  fairness and they demand policies that  help us create the kinds of jobs that we  desperately need in this economy but we  cannot continue to surrender it to the  Republicans who say yeah we have a  deficit problem but we don't want the  wealthiest people in this country  millionaires and billionaires to  contribute one penny more nor do we want  large profitable corporations that  contribute it has to come completely out  of the backs of working families the  elderly the sick and the children the  Democrats have got to say no that cannot  be the case that's not what the American  people want so I would hope the  Democrats hold firm and maybe for a  change the Republicans do some yelling  so if the Republicans were willing to  give up taxes and we're willing to to do  some of the things that you just talked  about would you be willing to see  reductions in Social Security Medicare  Medicaid as a trader always I think  there are all ways that you can move  toward deficit reduction but in fact I  don't think you have to hit those three  programs i think there are other  programs though i would prefer not to  see cut but i think may have to be cut  uh throughout across the board i will  tell you this that we have tripled  funding for defense since 1997 we're now  spending about a hundred and sixty  billion dollars a year still fighting  the wars in Iraq and Afghanistan we have  many weapons systems out there that were  designed to fight the old Cold War and  not international terrorism so I happen  to believe that we can make very  significant cuts in military spending as  part of deficit reduction while still  retaining our military strong military  presence there are other programs in  agriculture and across the board I think  you can cut but I don't know how you cut  Medicaid when you got 50 million people  today without  health insurance I don't know what you  do about Medicare Medicare you can make  some changes without reducing benefits  that I would be open to that for example  right now we don't negotiate drug prices  I think there's a lot of savings to be  made in Medicare if we did what we do  with the VA in negotiations with the  drug companies there are improvements  that can be made but the idea for  example that some have floated about  raising the eligibility level 267 is  basically insane what do you do if  you're 66 and your low income and you're  sick with cancer what does that mean  it's a death sentence and if I could not  support that senators and let me bring  you back to taxes as much as you say  that the Republicans are entrenched in  there no new taxes position colleague of  mine at Reuters spoke with Dave camp  yesterday the chairman of the House Ways  and Means Committee he said everything's  on the table as far as he's concerned  including tax policy Pat Toomey who is a  tea party favorite said yesterday that  there are quote indefensible tax breaks  that need to be looked at is there room  for some sort of maybe even symbolic tax  break closures here in this negotiation  well I would certainly hope so I'm glad  to hear some of these guys saying that  but I feared that at the end of the day  I may be wrong and I and I hope that  they are sincere about that but as you  well know the devil is in the details  what many of these guys believe in which  I don't is trickle-down economics and  that is a few lower tax rates for large  corporations which is what they want to  do uh that somehow magically we create  millions of jobs and everybody lives  happily ever after I happen not to  believe in the trickle-down economic  theory I think it benefits upper income  people but if they are open to do look  we know that there are large  multinational corporations who make  billions in profits who pay sometimes  nothing in taxes we know that we're  losing about a hundred billion dollars a  year through the wealthy and large  corporations stashing their money in the  cayman islands and in Bermuda and  another tax havens if these guys the  Republicans are willing to look at those  areas to raise revenue for our country  uh I would be very delighted and  certainly hope that that happens as you  know if this super committee does not  come up with an agreement there is a  trigger that kicks in where some  automatic spending cuts kick in about a  trillion dollars of additional spending  cuts and about half of that would be  from defense a lot of defense Hawks say  that would be cutting way too deeply but  perhaps you see it differently and I'm  wondering if you might prefer the the  trigger to be enacted rather than you  know sort of roll the dice with whatever  this bipartisan your so-called  supercommittee comes up with well it's a  question of rolling the dice what I  prefer the sequestration approach if you  ended up with a bad deal that cut Social  Security Medicare and Medicaid and  wrecked havoc with programs that working  families desperately need sure I would  prefer sequestration to that on the  other hand if the committee could come  up with a package that was fair and  responsible that called for shared  sacrifice that protected the needs of  ordinary people I would support that one  of the things that I've been really  concerned about I think you know the  media talks about a trillion here in a  trillion there we don't talk about what  these cuts mean to people in the real  world we're talking about the  possibility of massive cuts in child  care and in head start at a time when we  have a child care disaster we're talking  about massive cuts in college grants  when people can't afford to send their  kids to college we're talking about  decimating the Environmental Protection  Agency when we're trying to combat  global warming we're talking about cuts  and nutrition programs when we're seeing  an increase in hunger among children and  the elderly so I think we got to add  flesh and blood what are we really  talking about we're in the midst of a  horrendous recession people are hurting  and if you start cutting all of these  programs there's going to be pain and  there's going to be death so I think we  have to make sure that that does not  happen what did you think of the  Democratic Senate appointments to the  super committee patty Murray John Kerry  Max Baucus could Harry Reid have made  some better choices I think I thought I  heard you say earlier in the week you  would have liked to serve on the  committee yourself well you know I  I know you know John Max and Patty quite  well all of them are very very smart  their experience they are knowledgeable  my guess is that there will be a lot of  discussion between the committee members  and the caucus I don't think they're  going to be out there all on their own  and then coming up with a package I  think there's going to be discussion uh  and as I said earlier I hope at the end  of the day what they will be able to  accomplish is to get the Republicans  finally to understand that when our  country that has the most unequal  distribution of income and wealth of any  major country and that gap between the  very rich and everybody else is growing  wider that those folks on top are going  to have to contribute to deficit  reduction I hope I hope very much they  stay strong on that Senator the I think  in the one of the papers this week  called labeled Max Baucus a centrist  would you calm a centrist and if so what  does that mean to you in these  negotiations for the super committee  well look if you're asking me what I  have appointed three different people I  probably would have but you know max  baucus's chairman of the Finance  Committee he's a pretty smart guy I  think for the right reasons by the way  he was on the bowles-simpson Commission  as I recall he voted against it which I  think was the right vote but again I  think it's wrong to think that these  guys are going to be living in a world  of their own you know patty Murray is  the chair of the Senate Democrats on the  committee she is part of leadership I  think you're going to see a lot of  discussion taking place between the  caucus and those three folks um speaking  of Paul Simpson we have the bold Simpson  report which recommended reductions to  entitlements and increased tax revenue  you then had the Gang of Six proposal  which did the same sort of building on  bull Simpson and a similar type of  proposal and now you have President  Obama making the case almost on a  recently on a daily basis that we need  to compromise and have a balanced  package and in his mind what a balanced  package means is cuts and entitlements  and increases in tax revenues is  the president fundamentally wrong that  that's where the middle is and that's  where the compromise again you know I  think most Americans believe that in  fact the president and and the Democrats  have compromised far too much and the  Republicans have given virtually nothing  I think and you can correct me if i'm  wrong i think john boehner said after  this deficit reduction package was  passed that he got ninety eight percent  of what he wanted Paul Ryan said he got  seventy-five percent of what he wanted I  didn't hear any Democrats most thing  about what they got they didn't get  anything so I think the record in the  last half years is the Democrats have  compromised but let me get back to  President Obama when President Obama ran  for office what he said is vote for me  not John McCain because among other  reasons John became wants to cut your  Social Security benefits what the  president understands and has said and  his administration have said Social  Security hasn't contributed one penny to  our deficit Social Security compared  every benefit for the next 25 years  Social Security trust fund is a two  point six trillion dollar surplus why in  god's name would anybody including the  president talked about cutting social  security when if all we have to do is  lift the cap on people making $250,000  or more we can solve the problem for 75  years so if the president wants to do  that is going back on a campaign promise  and he's doing something which the  Republicans have always wanted to do so  what Bush talked about so I think that  that is a mistake so if you ask me about  compromise sure everybody has got to  compromise but when you have the richest  people in this country doing  phenomenally well corporate profits  phenomenally high wall street back to  where they were before they cause this  recession you know what that's to be a  whole lot of give on that side before  you tell the elderly and the sick and  the children who are hurting today that  they have got to give more so back in  december of last year you went on the  Senate floor and made a remarkable  speech lasted eight and a half hours I  think it was talking about these very  topics are you more or less frustrated  now than  you were then I mean if things got  better or worse between now and then  well I think if you read the book that  came out of that speech what you will  find that I and I say this a bit sadly  that I kind of predicted pretty much of  what would happen and what I said  essentially in December and the debate  in December the Republicans were saying  we will not extend unemployment benefits  to significant numbers of people who are  about to lose them and what ended up  happening is the president surrendered  on whether or not we would extend the  tax Bush tax breaks for the wealthy and  in fact also even lowered the tax rates  on estate taxes for the top three tenths  of one percent and what I said if when  you had in December a Democratic  president a Democratic Senate and then a  democratic house if you couldn't win  under those political circumstances how  in God's name but we got to win when  right-wing Republicans were controlling  the house unfortunately that is exactly  what happened in the Democrats have been  caving in and caving in since I think  most people do understand that so where  we are right now I think with our mop  the mystical pessimistic I think the  American people have to stand up very  clearly all over this country and say no  no you're not going to cut Social  Security let's be clear you know this  so-called chained CPI which they're  talking about you go out and you tell  seen yesterday do it say oh by the way  there are folks in Washington who think  your Cola today which you have not  received the last two years despite the  fact that your prescription drug costs  than your health care costs of risen  they think that's too generous and what  they want to do is wait if you're 65 now  when you're 75 take back about five  hundred and sixty dollars that otherwise  would have gotten and when you're 85 a  thousand dollars see how seniors feel  about that so this division on fiscal  policy between Democrats and Republicans  so deeply entrenched can we expect this  to carry on right into  the election campaign can Americans  expect many more months of this sort of  confrontation because both parties seem  intent on hashing this out right into  the campaign make and making the  election almost a referendum on their  positions is that your expectation well  I well I I think the truth is you have  very very different philosophies and  while needless to say I strongly  strongly strongly disagree with the  Republican ideology which is designed in  my view to protect the wealthiest people  in this country and the largest  corporations and is designed to take  away many of the programs that working  families have fought for for the last 80  years what I have to say is I do respect  their willingness to stand up and fight  they said that we will not support one  nickel of new taxes for millionaires and  billionaires and for large corporations  you know what they've kept their word I  think it's a disaster for the country  but they have to be complimented I  contrast that to some Democrats  including the president who said well  when I run for office this is what I'm  telling you but now guess what I've  changed my mind so I do think that you  have philosophical differences i think  trickle-down economic theory cuts in  Social Security Medicare Medicaid  infrastructure everybody in America  understands there's something  fundamentally wrong what we have an  infrastructure which is collapsing we're  not putting people to work rebuilding  that when we can create millions of  good-paying jobs everybody understands  that we can make huge progress  transforming our energy system into  energy efficiency and sustainable energy  not spend 350 billion a year importing  oil you know everybody most people  understand that our trade policies which  has resulted in shutdown after shutdown  of American plants we've lost fifty  thousand factories in the last ten years  millions of good-paying jobs now I know  my friends on the wall street journal  editorial page do not agree with me nor  in the New York Times editorial page go  out on Main Street and ask people how  they feel about the current trade  policies and what they've done to the  American economy where companies have  transferred millions of jobs to  don't know the third world countries we  need to be talking about in the  background though there are you know  these sort of huge economic and  financial events taking place Standard &amp;  Poor's downgrading us credit rating for  the first time in history these gigantic  swings in the stock market they're  chasing all sorts of investors  completely out of the market it's a  possibility that these events could just  overtake the political discussion here  in Washington and Joel people out of  their their partisan positions well you  know I don't like to what people need to  be discussing as a nation is where we  want to go uh again I I strongly  disagree with the Republicans but they  have been consistent about their beliefs  the American people are going to have to  decide as soon as possible whether they  really do believe that tax breaks for  billionaires and cuts in Social Security  and Medicare and Medicaid and a trade  policy which causes millions of jobs is  really what we can do as a nation and  survive economically so I would agree  with you that we are in the midst of a  great debate and I think these are  issues that have to be discussed you're  one of a number of prominent  progressives who have offered this  critique from the left of President  Obama but when President Obama's  advisors are asked about this and  whether they're concerned especially  heading into real the reelection  campaign whether the they're sort of  base voters will be there for them they  point to pulling that shows that really  his support among the most liberal  voters in the country is still very  strong vote among support among  Democrats to support among  self-identified liberals so what they  say is that this critique that you're  offering is really not felt broadly than  for the most part people are happy with  the people on the left are happy with  this performance so how do you respond  to that wait well I think one of the  problems is is what do we mean by a  liberal those polls may be right I'm not  a liberal never have been I'm a  progressive and there's a very big  difference between being a progressive  and liberal the average working class  person in the  country the average senior citizen is  probably not a liberal what they are are  people who in many cases are struggling  right now very very hard trying to  survive and I think if you go up to the  average senior or the average working  class person and you say to them the  president told you that he was not going  to cut social security but now he's  thinking about doing that or he's  thinking about raising the eligibility  age for Medicare 267 what do you think  and they with my guesses they will say  we're not happy with that we think that  that's a bad idea they're not liberals  on the other hand you know the president  I think has done a reasonably good job  on and let me you know i'm not here to  beat up the president these are really  tough times when he came into office we  losing 700,000 jobs a month uh you know  so I'm not here to just be beating up on  the president but I think the president  and is his people are making a big  mistake if they think that at the  grassroots level there are not a lot of  working-class people who are the bulk of  people in this country who are not angry  and frustrated in disillusioned I think  they're making a mistake you know when  the president has he done a good job on  gay rights more or less he has maybe he  could have done more has he done a good  job in women's rights he's appointed two  women to the Supreme Court a lot of  women are very proud of that I'm very  proud of that but I think if you ask  people on economic issues has he stood  up the wall street in my view you have  crooks on Wall Street and I use that  word advisedly who's illegal behavior  whose greed whose recklessness put us  into this terrible recession now they're  doing just great do you think the  average American thinks that the  president has stood up to Wall Street in  the way that he should have said I think  probably not so young say you're calling  on a grassroots movement you're calling  on Americans to stand up Kevin referred  to your eight and a half hour speech  that you gave recently or is there  something that's coming down the pike  down the road trade legislation or  something that you're eyeing up that  you're saying I'm not going to stand for  it I'm going to go back to the floor and  I'm going to filibuster again well I  don't know that I'm gonna be  filibustering again but you are going to  hear my voice very loudly I think on  a couple of issues number one it's very  important that ordinary Americans stand  up right now in every one of our 50  states and say to the Congress say to  the supercommittee say to the president  that the negotiations that are taking  place right now with regard to deficit  reduction be fair that this committee  not come up with the proposal that  continues to go after some of the most  vulnerable people in this country that's  issue number one and the trade issue i  think is enormous it's not something  that is discussed very much in the  corporate media because there's a  general consensus that corporate media  likes unfettered free trade but i think  if you go to grassroots america and you  ask people whether nafta cafta permanent  normal trade relations with china has  worked for the middle class and working  class in this country they will tell you  no one hasn't so that's another issue  that i will be a pretty active on you  you said senator that you're not here to  beat up on president obama but you have  in the past talked about a primary  challenge to president obama and spoken  approvingly of one and i'm wondering if  you know who you have in mind for that  challenge and what you think that that  might accomplish if well what no i don't  have I don't know of anybody in mind but  I'm sure that there are serious and  smart people out there who can do it  here's the point if you're asking me do  I think at the end of the day that  Barack Obama is going to be the  Democratic candidate for president in  2012 I do but do I believe that it is a  good idea for our democracy and for the  Democratic body and I speak by the way  as