u for being our  newsmakers appreciate it thank you very  much let me turn to our reporters here  and in just Kevin if I can begin with  you what you hear from the senator and  the prospects for the super committee  that the all the members have been named  and and they're going to start with  their work well he's clearly frustrated  with the way the White House has been  handling this deficit confrontation over  the last year or so the line that we're  getting from Democrats in from Bernie  Sanders an independent seems to be to  hold the line and to try and protect  Medicare Medicaid Social Security these  giant social programs that are so  important to progressives and Democrats  it's he did seem to be open to the idea  that there might be some promise for  maybe chiefly symbolic tax reform to  come out of this process but I don't  know that there's a pessimistic outlook  on that yeah yeah Laura go ahead  what I took from this is that most of  what the debate around this whole  deficit-reduction question has been has  been that the assumption that Democrats  are willing to compromise and  Republicans are not Democrats led by  President Obama have said no we're  putting entitlements on the table you  somewhat reluctantly leader Pelosi and  Majority Leader Reid have gone along  with that and of course republicans have  not what he's basically saying is no no  no that's that's not how it's going to  be you know I'm speaking up for for the  left wing of the Democratic Party and  I'm not willing to make those  compromises so whether his view will  prevail at the end of the day you know  I'm certainly not predicting that I  think that chances are if this committee  comes up with a what Obama would call a  balanced deal with taxes and  entitlements that that probably will go  through but I think what he's trying to  do is and this is I think the role that  he typically does play in the Congress  is to provide some countervailing  pressure from the left to say that you  know don't take my compromise for  granted you know I'm not ready to cut  Social Security benefits i'm not ready  to raise the age of eligibility for  Medicare and those are both things that  the president obama has said straight up  he's willing to do Joe he seemed to be  comfortable with the idea of way the way  the super committee is going to work in  that he repeatedly said they're going to  be talking to the caucus they're gonna  be talking to the to the leadership that  patty murray's part of leadership that  this committee this so-called super  committee is going to be operating  differently than what we saw the Gang of  Six doing that was well privately behind  the scenes they weren't talking to other  people about it I think that I mean I  think that that was abundantly clear  when the name started coming out this  week many of these people were drawn  from sort of the second tier of  leadership on both sides and so I think  it's pretty clear that they'll be having  their you know marching orders from the  highest levels of leadership in Congress  and it won't be one of these sort of  freelance deals like the Gang of Six was  where these guys were often a primary  man and in fact there are demands that  this process be very open there's been  legislation filed requiring that its  meetings be public that the members of  the committee disclosed their campaign  contributions faster than usual  these sorts of things I think that'll be  a very open process but for those who  are who are hoping for a deal the idea  that they'll be accountable the  leadership is not entirely a bad thing I  mean ultimately in order you know the  Gang of Six is all well and good you can  love it but it's not law you know if you  want to see something actually become a  law really the way that these deals have  been done is through the leadership so  if you have people in there who are  essentially speaking for their leaders  jon kyl speaking for Senator McConnell  or are patty Murray speaking for  something to read that is not that made  that may help make this happen so of  course you know the difference of the  super committee is that it is guaranteed  if they get a majority vote an  up-or-down vote in the Congress which no  other plans have had that have had that  privilege so this this starts with a leg  up but the ID that they're in there  representing leadership is not  necessarily mean that there can't be a  deal it means there can't be a deal and  less leader sign on and what about the  role the White House in this well the  White House doesn't have a direct role  in this committee I think that they're  more of an influencer in terms of  setting the tone I think that perhaps to  senator Sanders dismay the White House  has set a tone for the Democrats which  is you know where we are willing to  compromise I think it would be just  unforced just cannot see it happening  where Republicans come to the table and  say I'm willing to give you billions of  dollars in tax increases and Democrats  say no so I think that they've that  they're there really to set the tone I'm  sure that we White House aides in the  room who are there to essentially be  whispering into the ears of the  Democrats about where the lines can be I  mean they've gone through several rounds  of this already two very intense rounds  of negotiations with Speaker Boehner  where they got very close to a deal so I  think that this whole question is a lot  less about how do we do it where can we  cut and more where's the politics of  this what are we willing to do and the  numbers the ideas there's plenty out  there for them if they want to take it  and Kevin what about senator Sanders  relationship with Senate Majority Leader  Harry Reid or Democratic leadership in  general do they feel the pressure from a  senator Sanders well you know he's  pretty far out on the left  and so I'm not sure that they pay that  much attention to what he says but he  says it loudly he says it clearly and  often and so they can't help but you  know he can't help but get their  attention from time to time minute and I  think that he does but getting back to  the point where I was making I think you  know there is a pop pretty powerful of  political dynamic going on here and I  think what we're going to see is this  debate carrying right on into next year  and straight out to the election I think  you can pretty easily see the next  year's election being almost a  referendum on these two deeply divided  policy positions Kevin Drawbaugh with  Reuters Laura Meckler with Wall Street  Journal thank you both thank you thank  you
 

